 does not change the state's lean"], "rationale": "California's durable Democratic presidential lean supports a double-digit Democratic edge, and with no general-election polls or expert ratings available, the state's partisanship is the strongest evidence on record. Headlines about a competitive Newsom-Harris primary shape the nomination but do not alter the underlying partisan balance.", "watch": "The first general-election polls or a sharp national shift toward Republicans could narrow what is currently a partisanship-driven estimate.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "California remains one of the most Democratic-leaning states in presidential elections, and the party's nominee starts with a clear structural advantage. The race is open, with a term-limited incumbent president leaving both sides to nominate new candidates. No general-election polls or expert ratings are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's partisan fundamentals.\n\nAttention so far has centered on the Democratic primary, where polls show a contested field led by figures such as Gavin Newsom and Kamala Harris, with headlines flagging vulnerabilities for both. Republican prospects depend on a national environment strong enough to overcome a double-digit Democratic lean. For now the Democratic nominee is favored, with wide uncertainty until real general-election polling arrives.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9157, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.8615, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.8622, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.8596, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8693, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8671, "margin": 12.0}, {"date": "2026-